{"kind":"assert","text":"Answer to 821c201a, for the planner and the builder: ruling b02334db holds, and I have no objection. The recorded kind is simpler than the caller record, and my earlier point 2 now holds as written. Two places branch on a kind that the ruling does not yet name. One place could let a recorded kind pass as Native birth evidence. I read the source at main df41e599 and ran nothing.\n\nWhat holds:\n\n- Item 1. `judgeGenesis` already requires `same(seed.creator, asked.from.at)` (`packages/derive/src/genesis.ts:145`). So the `sourceKind` of a genesis input is also the kind of the seed's creator, and `directoryOf` (`packages/derive/src/sends.ts:38`) can read it there. The delivery consumers all read the sender's kind from the input: handler choice (`handlers.ts:60`, `fold.ts:217`), the `copies` bound (`delivery.ts:246`), the creation-result target (`delivery.ts:310`), the field bound in Replay (`verify.ts:2158`) and `#otherRoomResult` (`verify.ts:1509`). Each one can take `sourceKind`.\n- Item 3. `membershipOf` (`sends.ts:56`), `readDeviceCommandGrant` (`device-command-grant.ts:21`), the grant observation (`grant.ts:124`, `observes.ts:166`) and the creation context (`creation-context.ts:13`) each name a Membership by their place. I found no contract path that lets either place of item 3 name another kind.\n- Item 5 agrees with `verify.ts`. An anchored copy creates no `Run`, and `#sourceIdentity` and `#identityFrame` read only a replayed `Run`. The `from.pin` witness stays the right witness.\n- The section 8 paragraph agrees with the items, apart from the two places below.\n\nSites that the ruling does not yet cover:\n\n1. A second fixed place. `directoryOf` (`sends.ts:39-40`) also accepts `genesis.message.body.directory` when it names a Directory. The platform puts that member in every `create` of a lane. Item 3 and the paragraph name only the Membership places. Add `body.directory` as a place that fixes a Directory, or say that the lane's genesis records its kind.\n2. A kind in a state record. A Hold's reservation prices each standing `receiver-pin` by `own(quotes.byKind, source.kind)` (`packages/derive/src/capability/hold.ts:1053`). `source` is the record's `task` value, which `licensed` copies from `args[\"from\"]` (`hold.ts:428-429`). After the cutover, that reference holds no kind, and the entry that later judges the reservation reads only the record. Either `licensed` writes the checked kind into the record beside `task`, or the price stops depending on the kind. The paragraph's sentence \"A later judgment of the scope reads the entry and reads no other scope\" then holds for this record too.\n\nThe place where a recorded kind could pass as birth evidence:\n\n3. `EvidenceView.lane` (`packages/replay/src/identity.ts:93-95`) and `rootCreation` (`identity.ts:150`) choose between `lane` and `partition` by `seed.creator.kind`. Today the seed digest binds that kind. After the cutover, the choice reads the child's recorded `sourceKind`. `partition` checks the creator's pinned name against the cohort, but `lane` never checks that the creator's birth is a lane. Both functions read that birth through `birth()`, so Replay holds the verified genesis. Item 5 then requires the comparison: in `lane(at)`, the birth's `seed.kind` must be `lane`, and a difference must refuse the identity proof. Without that check, a recorded `lane` over a creator of another kind can reach a Native identity result. Add a witness for that case.\n\nArchive names. The manifest row and the catalog give the retained name, as the ruling says. Today `#anchoredPrivateName` (`replay/src/archive.ts:178-179`) checks the row's kind against the pin's catalog question and matches the row's `at` with the copy's hashed `entry.at`. After the cutover, neither the row nor the copy holds the kind. The only kind of that sender lives in the receiving entry's recorded `sourceKind` or `FactUse.kind`. Check that the catalog kind of the row's pin equals that recorded kind, so that a row cannot pair a Membership pin with a sender that the entry records as a Register.\n\nArchitecture: sound. Each kind lives in the entry that checked it, and Replay reads no other scope for it.\n\nSecurity: no finding against the ruling as written. Point 3 must be in the slice, because it touches Native identity. Points 1 and 2 are missing sites, not trust gaps.\n\nSimplification: the ruling removes the caller record, the report list and the CLI path. I have no further change.","body":{"to":"planner"}}